      },
      "body": "Thanks for your great work on veRL! However, the current implementation in SFT for grad accumulation seems wrong. The loss should be divided first, then do backward. Experiments show that fixing this bug can get loss 10% lower (from 2.7 -> 2.4) on some tasks",
      "changedFiles": 1,
      "comments": {
        "nodes": [
          {
            "author": {
              "__typename": "User",
              "login": "ZihanWang314"
            },
            "body": "Please also see the loss curve and grad norm curve here. With current implementation, grad norm is very high when micro batch size is smaller than batch size. Although AdamW automatically adapts different gradient scales, grad clipping will remove all grads more than 1.0 (it's 200 now!) and then the optimization will be disabled to have correct gradient information.\r\n\r\n<img width=\"588\" alt=\"image\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/4a0443de-2d20-4e35-877f-485fd46cae2f\" />\r\n<img width=\"534\" alt=\"image\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/0c056aca-da41-46d4-87b6-f770703f7be7\" />",
            "createdAt": "2025-03-04T20:41:42Z",
            "databaseId": 2698856034,
            "updatedAt": "2025-03-04T20:45:53Z",
            "url": "https://github.com/verl-project/verl/pull/436#issuecomment-2698856034"
          },
