  "inline_comments": [
    {
      "body": "Is it correct to perform a global grad clip after all the micro batches forward and backward?",
      "created_at": "2025-03-02T12:20:48Z",
      "diff_hunk": "@@ -394,8 +394,10 @@ def training_step(self, batch: TensorDict):\n         n_micro_batches = len(micro_batches)\n         step_loss = 0\n         for micro_batch in micro_batches:\n-            loss = self._compute_loss_and_backward(batch=micro_batch) / n_micro_batches\n+            loss = self._compute_loss_and_backward(batch=micro_batch, do_backward=False) / n_micro_batches\n+            loss.backward()\n             step_loss += loss.item()\n+            grad_norm = self.fsdp_model.clip_grad_norm_(max_norm=self.config.optim.clip_grad)",
      "html_url": "https://github.com/verl-project/verl/pull/436#discussion_r1976614209",
      "id": 1976614209,
      "line": 400,
      "original_line": 400,
      "path": "verl/trainer/fsdp_sft_trainer.py",
      "updated_at": "2025-03-02T12:20:48Z",
      "user": {
        "login": "vermouth1992"
      }
    },
    {
      "body": "Of course yes if the grad norm is divided first. Note that when every micro batch comes in, it only contributes to its fraction in the final batch, both for loss and for grad norm, so I'm very sure to implement correct micro-batching, each micro batch should be forward and backward first (with the loss divided before doing backward), then finally do the norm clip.",
      "created_at": "2025-03-04T20:38:54Z",
      "diff_hunk": "@@ -394,8 +394,10 @@ def training_step(self, batch: TensorDict):\n         n_micro_batches = len(micro_batches)\n         step_loss = 0\n         for micro_batch in micro_batches:\n-            loss = self._compute_loss_and_backward(batch=micro_batch) / n_micro_batches\n+            loss = self._compute_loss_and_backward(batch=micro_batch, do_backward=False) / n_micro_batches\n+            loss.backward()\n             step_loss += loss.item()\n+            grad_norm = self.fsdp_model.clip_grad_norm_(max_norm=self.config.optim.clip_grad)",
      "html_url": "https://github.com/verl-project/verl/pull/436#discussion_r1980194907",
      "id": 1980194907,
      "line": 400,
      "original_line": 400,
      "path": "verl/trainer/fsdp_sft_trainer.py",
      "updated_at": "2025-03-04T20:38:54Z",
      "user": {
        "login": "ZihanWang314"
      }
    },
    {
      "body": "I think we need to move the .backward() with in the context (https://github.com/volcengine/verl/pull/436/files#diff-07c65794985259af3f0f8e156fcf70119ad136cd42e06b4c6584799b1ebc8f2fR300)\r\n\r\notherwise SP will likely cause OOM issue",
      "created_at": "2025-03-04T20:50:56Z",
      "diff_hunk": "@@ -394,8 +394,10 @@ def training_step(self, batch: TensorDict):\n         n_micro_batches = len(micro_batches)\n         step_loss = 0\n         for micro_batch in micro_batches:\n-            loss = self._compute_loss_and_backward(batch=micro_batch) / n_micro_batches\n+            loss = self._compute_loss_and_backward(batch=micro_batch, do_backward=False) / n_micro_batches\n+            loss.backward()",
      "html_url": "https://github.com/verl-project/verl/pull/436#discussion_r1980210022",
      "id": 1980210022,
      "line": 398,
      "original_line": 398,
      "path": "verl/trainer/fsdp_sft_trainer.py",
      "updated_at": "2025-03-04T20:50:57Z",
      "user": {
        "login": "xingyaoww"
      }
    },
    {
      "body": "It's ok to perform backprop for each microbatch data, but I think the `clip_grad_norm` operation can be done after computing gradients for all micro-batches.\r\n\r\nSuggest change:\r\n```python\r\n        for micro_batch in micro_batches:\r\n            loss = self._compute_loss_and_backward(batch=micro_batch, do_backward=False) / n_micro_batches\r\n            loss.backward()\r\n            step_loss += loss.item()\r\n\r\n        grad_norm = self.fsdp_model.clip_grad_norm_(max_norm=self.config.optim.clip_grad)\r\n```",
      "created_at": "2025-03-05T06:41:48Z",
      "diff_hunk": "@@ -394,8 +394,10 @@ def training_step(self, batch: TensorDict):\n         n_micro_batches = len(micro_batches)\n         step_loss = 0\n         for micro_batch in micro_batches:\n-            loss = self._compute_loss_and_backward(batch=micro_batch) / n_micro_batches\n+            loss = self._compute_loss_and_backward(batch=micro_batch, do_backward=False) / n_micro_batches\n+            loss.backward()\n             step_loss += loss.item()\n+            grad_norm = self.fsdp_model.clip_grad_norm_(max_norm=self.config.optim.clip_grad)",
      "html_url": "https://github.com/verl-project/verl/pull/436#discussion_r1980793840",
      "id": 1980793840,
      "line": 400,
      "original_line": 400,
      "path": "verl/trainer/fsdp_sft_trainer.py",
      "updated_at": "2025-03-05T06:41:48Z",
      "user": {
        "login": "hiyouga"
      }
    }
  ],
  "issue_comments": [
    {
      "body": "Please also see the loss curve and grad norm curve here. With current implementation, grad norm is very high when micro batch size is smaller than batch size. Although AdamW automatically adapts different gradient scales, grad clipping will remove all grads more than 1.0 (it's 200 now!) and then the optimization will be disabled to have correct gradient information.\r\n\r\n<img width=\"588\" alt=\"image\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/4a0443de-2d20-4e35-877f-485fd46cae2f\" />\r\n<img width=\"534\" alt=\"image\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/0c056aca-da41-46d4-87b6-f770703f7be7\" />",
      "created_at": "2025-03-04T20:41:42Z",
      "html_url": "https://github.com/verl-project/verl/pull/436#issuecomment-2698856034",
      "id": 2698856034,
